workedExamples.solution_text:
(a) Keep the direction and multiply the length by $\frac{3}{2}$.
(b) Reverse the direction and halve the length.
(c) The zero vector has zero magnitude and no direction; represent it by a point.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=7mm,y=7mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{answerblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[->,thick,black] (0,0) -- (4,2);
\path (2,1) node[above left] {$\underset{\sim}{a}$};
\draw[->,thick,color=answerblue] (0,-2) -- (6,1);
\path (4,-0.5) node[below right] {$\frac{3}{2}\underset{\sim}{a}$};
\draw[->,thick,color=answerblue] (2,-4) -- (0,-5);
\path (1,-4.5) node[above left] {$-\frac{1}{2}\underset{\sim}{a}$};
\fill[color=answerblue] (5,-4) circle (1.5pt);
\path (5,-4) node[right] {$0\underset{\sim}{a}=\underset{\sim}{0}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

